# Where the remaining port-vs-C encode gap actually is — a per-class sizing

date: 2026-08-13 (evening session)
host: mac / Apple M4 Pro, 12 logical cores. Foreign load present throughout:
      a `qemu-system-x86_64` at ~18 % CPU (a concurrent agent's Linux op-trace
      container) plus that agent's builds. `~/bin/measlock` was NOT taken —
      nothing here is a timed A/B; see "What this is not" below.
port commit: aa6f64dca (profiling binary rebuilt from it, see below)
C reference: in-tree reference/svt-av1 @ v4.2.0, Bin/Release/libSvtAv1Enc.a
artifacts:  perf_class_attrib_2026-08-13.tsv (the tables)
            tools/perf_profile/{prof.sh,selftime.py,classify.py,attrib.py}

## The question this answers

`benchmarks/perf_gap_2026-08-13-r1r2.meta` puts the port at slope ratio p2 3.93x,
p6 3.27x, p10 2.89x, p13 2.89x, all cells byte-identical. The target is 1.03x.
`docs/C-VS-PORT-CODE-REVIEW-2026-08-13.md` ranked ideas from source structure but
measured nothing, and flagged "C has NEON kernels the port runs scalar" as a
candidate for the remaining multiple WITHOUT sizing it. This record sizes it.

## Method

* Both binaries run a steady-state loop of the SAME cell (the port harness's
  `warmup` argument; the C harness's likewise) and are sampled with
  `/usr/bin/sample <pid> <secs> 1`. The port's timed region and C's are >99 % of
  their respective profiles (port: `main -> encode_frame_420` 15316 of 15324
  samples; C: `main -> encode_once -> svt_av1_enc_send_picture` 6794 of 7126) —
  so per-cycle setup does NOT pollute either side. It was checked, not assumed.
* Node SELF time = node count - sum(children counts), summed per symbol over the
  whole tree (recursion-safe). Rust symbols demangled with `rustfilt`.
* `sample` prints `<deduplicated_symbol>` where an address maps to several
  linker-folded (ICF) names. On the C side that is 310 of 7126 samples at
  512² p10 — 4.4 %, the single largest unattributed block — and tracing its
  PARENT frames showed 290 of the 310 sit under `inv_txfm_*_neon`. Attributing
  by parent moves C's INV_TXFM from 0.125 ms to 0.232 ms and takes the
  port/C inverse-transform ratio from 2.01x to **1.08x**. Any reading of these
  profiles that leaves `<deduplicated_symbol>` unattributed will overstate the
  transform gap by ~2x. (The port's `<deduplicated_symbol>` lives in
  libsystem_malloc and is allocator time.)
* Per-class ms = (class self samples / total samples) × the PAIRED per-encode ms
  for that cell from `benchmarks/perf_gap_2026-08-13-r1r2.tsv` (n=9 interleaved
  randomised-order rounds, byte-identity verified). The profile supplies the
  SHARES; a real paired measurement supplies the SCALE. Nothing is extrapolated
  between sizes — 512² and 1024² were each profiled directly.
* Profiling binary: `CARGO_TARGET_DIR=target-perf RUSTFLAGS="-C debuginfo=1
  -C llvm-args=-align-all-functions=4"`, no `target-cpu=native`, separate target
  dir so the other agent's `target/` was never touched. Spot-checked against the
  stock release binary on the profiled cell: 6.44 ms vs 6.33 ms at 512² p10 —
  the debuginfo/alignment build is not measurably different work.

## What this is NOT

* Not a timed A/B. No claim here is a speedup. The shares are ratios within one
  process and are robust to the foreign load; the absolute ms come from the
  committed paired record, not from this session's clock.
* One profile per (cell, side). No repetition, no confidence interval on a share.
  Treat a 1-2 point difference between two classes as noise; the structure
  (which class is 20 % vs 2 %) is what is being asserted.
* bd8 4:2:0 still, `gradient` content, qp 40, `--lp 1`. The p6 numbers include
  loop restoration and CDEF; p10/p13 do not (both off at preset >= 7).

## RESULT 1 — the four buckets

Every port symbol was tagged against the 2026-08-13 SIMD census:
**SIMD_GAP** = the port's kernel is scalar-only on aarch64 AND C ships a
`SET_NEON`-registered counterpart; **SIMD_QUAL** = both sides have a real vector
arm; **ALLOC** = allocator + libc mem (memset/memmove/bzero, plus the xzone
allocator's own `mach_absolute_time`/`madvise`, verified by parent frame);
**SCALAR_BOTH** = driver, entropy writer, range coder and RDOQ code that is
scalar in C too.

| cell | ratio | SIMD_GAP | SIMD_QUAL | ALLOC | SCALAR_BOTH |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 512² p10 | 2.80x | +11.9 % | +17.3 % | +29.5 % | +41.3 % |
| 1024² p10 | 2.84x | +12.3 % | +17.1 % | +27.0 % | +43.6 % |
| 512² p6 | 3.10x | +16.0 % | +31.2 % | +21.7 % | +31.0 % |
| 512² p2 | 3.79x | +15.0 % | +26.9 % | +19.1 % | +39.1 % |

(share of the port-minus-C delta; 512² and 1024² p10 agree to ~2 points, which
is the only repeatability evidence in this record.)

**CORRECTION 2026-08-13 (later, same day).** The p6/p2 SIMD rows above are the
CORRECTED ones. `restoration::compute_stats` was first tagged SIMD_GAP on the
strength of the review's R8 "`mac_row_i32_neon` is dead on aarch64" note. That
note is about a DIFFERENT helper, used only by the AVX2 arm:
`compute_stats_impl_neon` (`restoration.rs:337`) is a real NEON kernel that
dot-products through `dot_i16_neon` (`:525`). It belongs in SIMD_QUAL. Moving it
took p6 SIMD_GAP 27.7 % -> 16.0 % and SIMD_QUAL 19.5 % -> 31.2 % (p2:
15.6 -> 15.0 and 26.2 -> 26.9; p10 is unaffected, restoration is off there).
**This strengthens the conclusion** — even less of the gap is missing coverage —
but it changes the p6 prescription: the biggest p6 item is a kernel that is
already vectorised on both sides and is 5.1x slower than C's, so it needs a
BETTER kernel, not a first one. The other three p6 items
(`wiener_convolve_add_src`, `cdef_find_dir`, `compute_cdef_dist_8bit`) were
re-verified scalar-only by direct source read.

**The headline: at the fast presets where the 2.89x lives, missing SIMD coverage
is ~12 % of the gap.** Driving every scalar-where-C-is-NEON kernel to C's cost
takes 512² p10 from 2.80x to **2.47x**. Adding perfect parity on the kernels that
are ALREADY vectorised on both sides (SIMD_QUAL, where the port is 1.95-2.20x
slower than C's NEON) takes it to **2.27x**. Adding a zero-allocation port takes
it to **1.74x**. The remaining 41-44 % is the port being 2.2-2.4x slower than C
at code neither side vectorises.

So: **1.03x is not reachable through SIMD work, and not reachable through SIMD
plus allocation either.** To hit 1.03x every bucket must reach parity AND the
SCALAR_BOTH bucket must additionally shrink from 3.48 ms to ~1.66 ms at 512²
p10 — a 2.10x speedup of the port's driver/entropy/RDOQ code on top of
everything else. Nothing measured here suggests a mechanism for that.

## RESULT 2 — per class, 512² p10 (port ms / C ms / port over C)

| class | port | C | ratio | share of gap |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| MD_DRIVER (evaluate_leaf, tx_unit_inner, pd0, partition) | 1.437 | 0.375 | 3.84x | 23.1 % |
| ALLOC | 1.020 | 0.040 | 25.4x | 21.3 % |
| COEFF_CTX (nz-map ctx, cost_coeffs_txb, fill_levels) | 0.778 | 0.034 | 22.8x | 16.2 % |
| DISTORTION (hadamard/satd, sse, residual, variance) | 0.691 | 0.100 | 6.90x | 12.8 % |
| LIBC_MEM | 0.590 | 0.174 | 3.39x | 9.0 % |
| FWD_TXFM | 0.616 | 0.236 | 2.61x | 8.2 % |
| SYNTAX_WRITE | 0.281 | 0.025 | 11.4x | 5.6 % |
| INTRA_PRED | 0.337 | 0.103 | 3.28x | 5.1 % |
| RANGE_CODER | 0.299 | 0.239 | 1.25x | 1.3 % |
| QUANT_RDOQ | 0.401 | 0.357 | 1.13x | 1.0 % |
| INV_TXFM | 0.251 | 0.232 | **1.08x** | 0.4 % |
| COEFF_WRITE | 0.303 | 0.396 | **0.77x** | -2.0 % |

Three classes are already at or past parity: the inverse transform (R1's gate did
its job), the RDOQ/quantizer, and the coefficient WRITER — the port's
`write_coeffs_txb_1d` is 23 % FASTER than C's `av1_write_coeffs_txb_1d`. The
range coder is 1.25x. **Nothing in the entropy/quant column is a lever any more.**

At 512² p6 the shape changes: LOOP_RESTORE +15.6 % (5.72x) and CDEF +12.3 %
(7.82x) appear and are together 28 % of the p6 gap — and both are almost purely
coverage: `restoration::compute_stats` 3.83 ms vs `svt_av1_compute_stats_neon`
0.75 ms, `restoration::wiener_convolve_add_src` 1.04 ms vs
`svt_av1_wiener_convolve_add_src_neon` 0.10 ms (10.3x), `cdef::cdef_find_dir`
0.79 ms vs `cdef_dir_from_lines_neon` 0.05 ms (15x). That is where SIMD work pays.

## RESULT 3 — a finding that is NOT a SIMD gap: the MDS0 Hadamard

At 512² p10 the port spends **0.343 ms (4.79 % of its whole frame)** and at
1024² p10 **1.282 ms (5.12 %)** in `leaf_funnel::hadamard_satd` +
`dsp::hadamard::{hadamard_col8, aom_hadamard_8x8/16x16/32x32}`.

**C spends zero.** Not "a little": `grep -ci 'hadamard|satd'` over C's entire
sampled call graph returns **0** at 512² p10 (7,126 samples) and **0** at
1024² p10 (19,073 samples), while `svt_aom_variance{8x8,16x16,32x32,64x64}_neon_dotprod`
DO appear in both. That is not a per-unit speed difference — no scalar-vs-NEON
ratio produces 0 samples against the port's 5 %.

Read from the C source, this is C's `fast_loop_core` taking a DIFFERENT ARM:

    product_coding_loop.c:1283   } else if (ctx->mds0_use_hadamard_blk) {   // hadamard_path
    product_coding_loop.c:1296   } else { ... fn_ptr->vf(...)  }            // variance
    product_coding_loop.c:9473   ctx->mds0_use_hadamard_blk =
                                     ctx->mds0_use_hadamard_sb && fast_candidate_total_count > 1;

`mds0_use_hadamard_sb` is `true` on the all-intra path (`enc_mode_config.c:8148`,
inside `svt_aom_sig_deriv_enc_dec_allintra`), so the live term is
`fast_candidate_total_count > 1`. At preset >= 9 the port's own funnel arms the
`dc_only` gate (`FunnelCfg::dc_only_gate`, `leaf_funnel.rs:1078`) which injects
exactly `{DC_PRED}` — one candidate — so C's condition is false and C takes the
variance arm. The port has no such gate: `leaf_funnel.rs:4923` computes the
Hadamard SATD unconditionally.

Both arms then apply the same `<< 4` (C :1306) and feed the same fast cost, and
the port currently passes 168/168 byte-identity while producing a DIFFERENT
`luma_fast_dist` from C in exactly these blocks — which is itself the proof that
the value is inert wherever the candidate count is 1.

This is the same class as the review's R1 and R2 (work whose result cannot reach
the bitstream), not a SIMD-coverage item, and it is the largest single one left
at the presets the headline gap is quoted at. The fix is C's gate plus C's
variance arm, not a NEON Hadamard — a NEON Hadamard would still be strictly more
work than C does.

The falsifier, if anyone wants to re-run it: a call census bucketed by injected
candidate count. If most `hadamard_satd` calls at p10 have count > 1, the gate
will not fire and the win is not there.

## What else the profiles say, recorded so it is not re-derived

* `coeff_c::nz_map_ctx` is 0.374 ms at 512² p10 (5.2 % of the port's frame) and
  **784 of its 800 self samples have `quant::optimize_b` as their parent** — it
  is the RDOQ trellis's per-coefficient context derivation, not the coefficient
  writer's. C does the same work scalar inside `svt_aom_quantize_inv_quantize`.
  Not a SIMD-coverage item; a candidate for the incremental-context trick.
* `intra_pred::predict_dc` is 0.314 ms at 512² p10 / 1.073 ms at 1024² p10
  (4.3 %) and is scalar; C's `svt_aom_dc_predictor_*_neon` total ~0.005 ms.
  This IS a clean coverage gap. `intra_pred.rs` carries exactly one `incant!`
  (PAETH); everything else in the file is scalar.
* The xzone allocator charges its own `mach_absolute_time` (210 samples at 512²
  p10) and `madvise` to libsystem_kernel; a profile that classes those as
  "syscall" understates ALLOC by ~1.4 points.
* C burns 233 samples (0.084 ms, 3.3 % of its 512² p10 frame) in
  `build_nmv_component_cost_table` — motion-vector cost tables on an all-intra
  still. The port does not. It is one of the few places C does work the port
  does not, and it is why C's PIPELINE class is not near zero.
